  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14245089/assets/99212835e0eec95ac4ebd0c7d0fd49e1bb7e1a392f53251aa49510ac8ef5b65d.png)\n\nWhich of the above quadrilaterals are similar to $T$ (other than $T$ itself)?",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that $T$ is a right trapezoid with one pair of parallel opposite sides.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given polygons.\n\n- $K$ is *not* similar to $T.$ The polygon $K$ is a right trapezoid, and the corresponding angles are congruent, but the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are not the same: \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{14}\\ne \\frac{6}{6}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* similar to $T$ because $R$ is not a trapezoid (it's a rectangle since it has $2$ pairs of opposite parallel sides, and $2$ pairs of right angles).\n\n- $M$ is similar to $T.$ Indeed, $M$ is a right trapezoid. Furthermore, the corresponding angles are congruent, and the ratios of the lengths of the corresponding sides are the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{14}=\\frac{3}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$M$ only\".",
